					<p class="body1" align="left"><span class="body1 style73"> <h2>News archive</h2><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-28 | Tuesday</font></strong><br><br><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">&quot;A hero serves the people to his very last breath.&quot;</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; min-height: 13px; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">- Jose Maria Sison</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; min-height: 13px; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">If you read the liner notes in the <span style="font-weight: bold" class="Apple-style-span">Bayani</span> CD case, you might have noticed the quote above. You might have even seen me rock a shirt with the same quote or with <span style="font-weight: bold" class="Apple-style-span">Jose Maria Sison&#39;s</span> (JoMa) image. Alot of the work that I do, whether through music or organizing, is influenced by JoMa Sison. It was and still is his book <span style="font-style: italic" class="Apple-style-span">Philippine Society and Revolution</span> (penned as Amado Guerrero) that equipped me with the analysis of not only why we live in these conditions, but how to change it.</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; min-height: 13px; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">On August 27, Sison was arrested on charges for murders that occurred in the Philippines while he sat in exile in the Netherlands (<a href="../smf/index.php?topic=3179.0" title="RELEASE JOMA SISON">read press release here</a>). Clearly a political move, the Dutch government has been under constant pressure by the US and Philippines to discredit and attack JoMa Sison for his organizing work and political stance. </p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; min-height: 13px; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">Just like the nearly 900 political activists in the Philippines who have been assassinated or abducted under the current Gloria Macapagal-Arroyo regime, or the homie Freedom Allah Siyam who is being harassed by the FBI here in Seattle - JoMa&#39;s only crime is that of serving the people rather than serving the interests of those in power.</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">Stay informed and spread the word. The clock is ticking.</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; min-height: 13px; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">For more information, check:</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">www.bayanusa.org</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">www.josemariasison.org</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; min-height: 13px; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">RELEASE JOMA SISON NOW!</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; min-height: 13px; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">Isulong,</p><p style="font-family: Verdana; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 11px; line-height: normal; margin: 0px">Geo</p><p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-20 | Monday</font></strong><br><br><div align="center"><strong>NEW DOWNLOAD TO YOUR UPPER RIGHT</strong><br /></div><br />i made this beat a while back.  nobody wanted to rap on it.  so instead, i&#39;m making it available for download for you folkers.  hate it or love it.<br /><br />saba<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-16 | Thursday</font></strong><br><br>I will state this from the jump: I&#39;m mad as hell. Maaad. Nothing kills me more than when my equipment fails. Most producers will tell you that the key to productivity is a functional and effecient workflow (that and, ummm, talent)...nothing kills the efficieny like things that break...<br /><br />I use a combination of software and hardware tools, but most of my sequencing and prgramming is done &quot;inside the box&quot;..that is to say that I handle my business within the confines of a computer screen. Programming drums and samples goes a bit like this: I hit a pad or a key on one of two midi controllers, and a midi note shows up in my sequencer. Kicks and snares. Crazy.<br /><br />Midi keyboards like this one are pretty easy to come by, and work well. The reality, however, is that keys ain&#39;t so great when it comes to percussion. Enter: the midi pad controller. Fashioned after the pads from the venerable MPC, most pad controllers have a bank of 16 different pads, each assignable to a different note, drum sound, or sample. Hit a pad, hear a sound. Pretty simple? Never. These things vary like you wouldn&#39;t believe in terms of sensitivity, feeling, accuracy, and overall dopeness. I&#39;ve messed with just about all of these things, from the original Akai MPD16, to the M-Audio Trigger Finger, to my current joint, the Korg padKontrol. Banging on the MPD16 was like trying to tap your fingers repeatedly against a pile of bricks. it hurt. <br /><img src="http://www.maketunes.com/images/mpd16.jpg" alt="" width="450" height="346" /><br />The Trigger Finger was marginally better, but the pads were so damn soft that I felt like I had to hit the thing as hard as I possibly could to ensure that it registered my input. Not fun.<br /><img src="http://www.synthtopia.com/news/06_01/images/M-Audio_Trigger_Finger_Live_Bundle.jpg" alt="" width="350" height="283" /><br />My most recent acquisition was the padKontrol, which was all gravy and banana splits until yesterday, when two of the pads straight died. I&#39;ve had it for two months. <br /><img src="http://www.portlandmusiccompany.com/padkontrol_pics/padkontrol.jpg" alt="" width="432" height="319" /><br />I&#39;m now forced to choose between going back to banging out half-cocked rhythms on my weighted keyboard, or try out the new Akai MPD24. Akai&#39;s MPC pads are the monsters of the bunch, but the company has seemed unable to transfer those pads into their line of Midi controllers. Weak sauce. I&#39;m dubious, but I think Ima have to make the jump, unless convinced otherwise. I needs me some pads.<br /><img src="http://thumbnail.image.rakuten.co.jp/@0_mall/creators/cabinet/new-akai/mpd24.jpg" alt="" width="423" height="273" /><br />Maybe it&#39;s time to say fuck it, buy an EPS and an SP1200, and go all RZA on you fools...37 Chambers what?? <br /><img src="http://www.wutang-corp.com/images/photos/rza1.jpg" alt="" width="396" height="337" /><p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-16 | Thursday</font></strong><br><br>There&rsquo;s been a lot of talk this year about how <strong>hip-hop</strong> causes <strong>violence</strong> and is connected to <strong>crime</strong>
and, quite frankly, I&rsquo;m getting tired of the debate around this issue.
I&rsquo;d like to put my two cents in. Now, those of you who are familiar
with my reputation are probably thinking that I&rsquo;m gonna try to defend
this hip-hop stuff. No sir. In fact, I commend all journalists for
making even the most outlandish links between hip-hop and violence in
this town and further, I&rsquo;ll verbally stomp people for criticizing them.
For example, there was an <strong><a href="http://seattlepi.nwsource.com/local/327293_shooting11.html">article</a> </strong>on <strong>seattlepi.com</strong> that reported a shooting on First Avenue on August 10th.  The article also mentioned that a hip-hop show featuring <strong>Devin The Dude</strong> was <em>scheduled to perform a block away</em> from the incident.  What shocked me were the <a href="http://seattlepi.nwsource.com/soundoff/comment.asp?articleID=327293"><strong>comments</strong></a>
that people made in response to this article on the website.
Individuals from the community were talking all types of shit about how
these two things&mdash;a shooting and a hip-hop show scheduled a block
away&mdash;had nothing to do with each other! Were these people trying to
defend hip-hop? Why?! Can&rsquo;t they recognize that hip-hop is clearly a
driving force behind violence? <br />
<br />
I decided to conduct my own investigation of the matter with local musician and comedian <strong>Ahamefule Oluo</strong>.
It may surprise some of you that we turned up evidence clearly
implicating hip-hop&rsquo;s ties to several reported and unreported crimes in
the <strong>Beacon Hill</strong> neighborhood.  What follows is a photo journal of our sleuthing.  

<u><strong><br />
<br />
ASSAULT:</strong></u><strong>  </strong>A resident on <strong>Pearl Street </strong>told us of
an incident that occurred last June. What began as an argument over a
parking spot between two men, escalated into an altercation that ended
with one of the men getting stabbed in the eye with a fork.<br />
&nbsp;<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/pic1.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
Although a written confession and thirteen witness accounts of the
incident gathered by police all corroborate this story, we dug deeper
and found that the culprit had a cousin who was a <strong>rapper</strong> with a <strong>mixtape</strong> <em>scheduled for release two weeks later</em>.  Hmm, did somebody say <strong>hip-hop</strong>?

<u><strong><br />
<br />
ROBBERY:</strong></u>   We visited a corner store just a few blocks from Pearl Street and interviewed a clerk who worked there.<br />
&nbsp;<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/pic2.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
A man the police claimed to have been a <em>&ldquo;drug addict&rdquo;</em> stealing money to <em>&ldquo;pay for drugs&rdquo;</em>
had robbed the store at gunpoint in May. When pressed for more
information, the clerk repeatedly told us the crime had been solved and
the perpetrator was in jail&hellip; but was he? Or was <em>it?<br />
</em>&nbsp;<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/pic3.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
After looking around the building for just 10 minutes we discovered <strong>graffiti</strong> on the wall!  Proof that <strong>hip-hop</strong> was connected!

<br />
<br />
<strong><u>KIDNAPPING:</u></strong>   Earlier that day, there was a child reported missing.  He was last seen riding his bike at the <strong>Maplewood Play Field</strong>.<br />
&nbsp;<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/pic4.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
My colleague and I went to the scene to find nothing but a lonely
bicycle accompanied only by the absence of a child&rsquo;s laughter&hellip;
<br />
<br />
<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/pic5.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
&hellip;and a <strong>boombox</strong>! An item dearly coveted by none other than <strong>HIP-HOP</strong>!  We could smell it in the air.

<strong><u><br />
<br />
MURDER:</u></strong> Just when we thought the evidence against hip-hop
couldn&rsquo;t be more convincing, something caught my attention out of the
corner of my eye. It was a <strong>tree</strong>.<br />
&nbsp;<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/pic6.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
A <em><strong>dead tree</strong></em>.  Could it be&hellip; foul play? I shuddered to think of how this unwitting tree had its youth cut short by a murderous villain. 

<br />
<br />
<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/pic7.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
No&hellip; it can&rsquo;t be&hellip; dare I look?<br />
&nbsp;<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/pic8.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
Just beyond the thicket we found&hellip;<br />
&nbsp;<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/pic9.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
&hellip;<strong>heavily-sampled records from the 70&rsquo;s</strong>!  I couldn&rsquo;t believe my eyes, and yet, I couldn&rsquo;t deny the evidence that the <em>death of this tree was inextricably linked to <strong>hip-hop</strong></em>.  Possibly even local hip-hop.<br />
&nbsp;<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/pic10.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
<br />
Back home, an online investigation uncovered more evidence of hip-hop&rsquo;s indisputable connections to <strong>assassination</strong>, <strong>insider trading</strong>, and <strong>crimes against humanity</strong>.<br />
&nbsp;<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/kennedy.jpg" alt="" />
<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/kenlay.jpg" alt="" />
<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/hitlerfatboys.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
As far as I&rsquo;m concerned, the media is on the right track with this one.  Keep searching for the truth <em>with the same integrity you&rsquo;ve exhibited thus far</em>, my friends!  There will be no rest for the weary until we have pigeonholed <strong>hip-hop </strong>as the <strong>root of evil</strong> it is and gotten it safely away from our streets and the ears of our children.<br />
&nbsp;<img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/hiphopviolence/polar.jpg" alt="" />

<br />
<br />
